+# The audit chain's head, anchored outside the database
+
+Security plan **P0-8**, the anchored half (finding DP-04). Launch rows **D5**
+and **D6**, 2026-10-01.
+
+P0-8's acceptance: *"truncating the newest rows fails the anchored
+verification."*
+
+## What was wrong
+
+`audit_logs` is a sha256 chain, one per tenant (`server/services/audit/chain.ts`).
+A chain detects a row changed or removed from its middle, because every later
+link stops deriving. It cannot detect two other things:
+
+- **The newest rows removed.** What remains is a valid, shorter chain.
+- **The database restored from an older backup.** The chain is also valid,
+  just older.
+
+Triggers refuse UPDATE, DELETE and TRUNCATE, and since the grant half of P0-8
+(`06152498b`) the runtime role holds none of those privileges. That stops the
+application. It does not stop the database owner, a superuser, or a restore.
+The daily sweep (`server/jobs/auditChainIntegritySweep.ts`) verified every
+store, but only against the database itself, so none of these three could
+show up in it.
+
+`tests/db/audit-chain-anchor.dbtest.ts` shows the gap directly. After a
+tenant's two newest rows are removed, `verifyAuditChain` returns `ok: true`.
+
+## What changed
+
+**`server/services/audit/chain-anchor.ts`**
+
+- **The anchor.** Once a day the sweep writes each tenant's head (row id,
+  `chain_seq`, `sha256_chain`, `created_at`) to the object-locked evidence
+  bucket.
+  - The anchor is canonical JSON with its own digest.
+  - There is one key per UTC day: `audit-chain-anchors/YYYY/MM/DD.json`.
+- **The check.** On every run the sweep checks every head that any anchor
+  names:
+  - still in `audit_logs` with the same `sha256_chain`: **ok**;
+  - gone, but a batch the archive door recorded (`audit_log_archives`) spans
+    its `created_at`: **accounted for**;
+  - otherwise: **broken**. The newest rows were removed, the database went
+    back in time, or the head was rewritten.
+- **Why every anchor, not only the latest.** The task can write anchors, so it
+  could write a newer one that names an older head after truncating. An older
+  anchor still names the removed head, and that anchor cannot be replaced:
+  - each key is written once (`If-None-Match: *`);
+  - Object Lock keeps every version;
+  - the reader reads every version and every delete marker, and treats a
+    second version or a delete marker on an anchor as **broken**.
+
+  So a forged anchor can raise a false alarm, but it cannot hide a removal.
+- **When an anchor is written.** Only when the chain verified and no anchor
+  check failed, so a tampered state is never anchored.
+- **The store.** `S3AnchorStore` reads `AUDIT_ANCHOR_BUCKET` and, optionally,
+  `AUDIT_ANCHOR_PREFIX`. Without them the check is **unverifiable**, never ok.
+
+**The sweep.** A new store, `audit_logs.anchor`, sits beside the six existing
+ones and uses the same verdicts. A broken anchor check raises the same alert
+path: the error log, `process.emitWarning` and the `[SECURITY]` webhook. The
+alert names a tenant and a row id, never content.
+
+**`ci:tenant-entry-points`.** The sweep's justification was re-read: anchoring
+is one more thing that must not stop for a suspended or pending-deletion
+tenant. A dated note was added and only this entry's digest was refreshed.
+
+**Terraform**
+
+- **`terraform/modules/audit-anchor-access`** (new, with its module test, and
+  added to CI's Terraform matrix). It grants the task role:
+  - `PutObject` only with `If-None-Match: *`;
+  - `GetObject`, `GetObjectVersion` and `ListBucketVersions` under the prefix;
+  - the evidence key, only through S3 and only for this bucket.
+
+  It grants no delete, retention, legal-hold or governance-bypass action.
+- **`terraform/modules/compliance-evidence`** exports `kms_key_arn`.
+- **`terraform/stack/audit_anchor.tf`** instantiates the module on the task
+  role.
+
+## Shown
+
+All database runs are on PostgreSQL 16.13, on a database built by
+install-fresh + deploy-migrate. The rows are written by the production writer
+(`writeChainedAuditRow`). The removals are made as the database owner with the
+trigger disabled, the actor this check exists for. The bucket in the dbtest is
+an in-memory stand-in with Object Lock's semantics: a key is written once, and
+every version and delete marker stays visible. The S3 store itself is covered
+by the unit test.
+
+| Case | Before (`red/`) | After (`green/`) |
+|---|---|---|
+| The newest rows removed: the chain still verifies, the anchor check fails and names the tenant and row | nothing compares the chain to anything outside it (`red/no-anchor-check.txt`) | broken |
+| A newer anchor naming the older head does not hide the removal | — | broken |
+| A head rewritten in place | — | broken |
+| An anchor written twice, or hidden behind a delete marker | — | broken |
+| A head removed by the archive door: accounted for by the door's record, and only by it | — | broken without the record, ok with it |
+| The first run anchors every tenant's head; the next checks them and anchors again; the same day writes nothing | — | green |
+| A chain that did not verify is not anchored | — | green |
+| The sweep runs the check, reports it as its own store, alerts on broken, and is unverifiable without a bucket | 6 red at HEAD's sweep (`red/sweep-at-head.txt`) | 24/24 |
+| S3: `If-None-Match: *` and a SHA-256 checksum on the put, a 412 reported as existing, every version and delete marker read across pages, the oldest body returned; an anchor written under the prefix the store lists | — | 7/7 |
+
+Green runs:
+
+- `green/dbtest.txt`: 8/8.
+- `green/db-tier.txt`: the whole real-database tier, 104 files and 1013
+  tests, all pass.
+- `green/unit.txt`: 31/31.
+- `green/terraform.txt` (Terraform 1.9.8, the CI version, with the AWS
+  provider mocked):
+  - the module: 2/2;
+  - the stack, with the grant in it: 36/36;
+  - `compliance-evidence`: 4/4.
+
+**Mutations.** Each was applied, run, seen red, and restored.
+
+m9 is the defect a review of this change found before it was committed: with
+`AUDIT_ANCHOR_PREFIX` set, anchors were written where the check never read them
+and where the grant does not reach. The store now owns its prefix.
+
+`mutations.txt`, the application:
+
+| # | Mutation | Red |
+|---|---|---|
+| m0 | No check (HEAD) | truncation, forged anchor, rewrite, archive, steady state |
+| m1 | Only the latest anchor checked | the forged-anchor case |
+| m2 | A second version not looked at | the written-twice case |
+| m3 | Delete markers not looked at | the hidden case |
+| m4 | An absent head always counts as archived | truncation, forged anchor, archive "only by it" |
+| m5 | The stored hash not compared | the rewrite case |
+| m6 | A chain that did not verify anchored anyway | that case |
+| m7 | The oldest row anchored instead of the newest | truncation, forged anchor |
+| m8 | The put not conditional | the S3 unit case |
+| m9 | The key written under the default prefix rather than the store's own | the configured-prefix case |
+
+`terraform-mutations.txt`, the grant. Each one fails the assertion written
+for it:
+
+| # | Mutation | Fails at the module test's line |
+|---|---|---|
+| t1 | The put not conditional | 26 |
+| t2 | `s3:DeleteObject` granted | 36 |
+| t3 | Reads across the whole bucket | 50 |
+| t4 | The key usable for any bucket | 65 |
+| t5 | The listing not confined to the prefix | 50 |
+| t6 | The server told another variable name | 82 |
+
+## Not done, and why
+
+- **The task does not receive the variables yet.** The task environment is
+  assembled by the two `concat(...)` lines in `terraform/stack/main.tf`. That
+  file is inside the D1 production-blockers lane's claim (`…01SuVLo2`), so the
+  one-line change is handed to it in `docs/work-orders/README.md`. Until it
+  lands, production reports `audit_logs.anchor` as unverifiable every day,
+  never as passed.
+- **Needs the apply to show.**
+  - S3 accepting the `s3:if-none-match` condition key in an identity policy.
+    If it did not, the put would be refused, and the sweep reports the
+    refusal as `error`.
+  - The KMS encryption context with bucket keys on. A mock cannot show either.
+- **The archive record is trusted for a head that is gone.** A superuser could
+  insert a false batch into `audit_log_archives` to cover a removal. That row
+  is itself append-only, and it names a locator and a sha256 that can be
+  checked against the cold archive. Checking the archived row inside the cold
+  object is not done here.
+- **Only `audit_logs` is anchored.** `audit_events` and
+  `audit.tamper_proof_log` are verified by the sweep, but their heads are not
+  anchored. The same module can take them.
+- **Cost.** Each run reads every anchor and checks every distinct anchored
+  head in one statement: about 3,650 small reads and one query after ten
+  years. A checkpointed summary can replace that when it matters.
